\begin{lemma}[Exactness of the state key]\label{lem:key}
Two canonical states of the form \eqref{eq:state-form} represent the same
element of \(\PSU(1,1)\) at \(U=u=e^{-1/10}\) if and only if their stored
parity, exponent, and canonical numerator coincide.
\end{lemma}

By \eqref{eq:length-trace}, radius \(111/10\) is equivalent to
\(|\alpha|^2<\cosh(111/20)^2\).  Rational positive-series enclosures give
\begin{equation}\label{eq:guard}
\begin{aligned}
 \cosh(111/20)^2
 &\in(16543.29004587223200052996283,\\
 &\hspace{2.5em}16543.29004587223200052996285)<20000.
\end{aligned}
\end{equation}
The deliberately rounded-up integer 20000 is the centre guard used by the
enumerator.

\begin{proof}
The exact finite execution described in Section~\ref{sec:implementation}
exhausts \(\mathcal C\).  For each of its 18,532 nonidentity elements, it
computes \eqref{eq:H}.  There are 18,388 strict positive signs and 144 exact
zero polynomials; no negative sign occurs.  Hence every nonidentity element
of \(\mathcal C\) has length at least \(\length_*\).

